IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `eca-gateway`

-- Employment Claims Act 2016 (Singapore) -- Part 3, the tribunal's jurisdiction
-- and the rules that govern a claim once the gateway in Part 2 has been passed.
--
-- ss 12 to 16, 21, 22, 23, 27 and 28. Division 1 of Part 3 (ss 8 to 11, the
-- establishment of the tribunals and the appointments) is not encoded: it
-- constitutes bodies and decides nothing.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules
-- skill. No pipeline, no independent test pass, no human gate.

§ `Employment Claims Act 2016 -- Part 3`

§§ `12 -- jurisdiction`

-- s 12(1): "Subject to this Act, a tribunal has jurisdiction to hear and
-- determine any claim in relation to which ALL APPLICABLE REQUIREMENTS set out
-- in subsections (2) to (7) are satisfied, REGARDLESS WHETHER the dispute
-- giving rise to the claim arose before, on or after 1 April 2017."
--
-- Six requirements, all of them cumulative.
--
-- s 12(2): the claim must be made --
--   (a) by a PRESCRIBED employee (or a person entitled under written law to the
--       amount in the employee's place) against that employee's employer;
--   (b) by such an employee against a person liable under Employment Act 1968
--       s 65 for the employee's salary; or
--   (c) by a PRESCRIBED employer against an employee, and then only about
--       "any matter specified in item 17 of the First Schedule or item 14 or 16
--       of the Second Schedule", or any other Schedule matter prescribed for
--       the purpose.
--
-- s 12(2)(c) is the whole of the employer's access to this tribunal, and all
-- three named items are money an employee owes for leaving without notice or
-- breaking the contract: salary in lieu of notice, the Employment Act s 11(1)
-- payment, and the Employment Act s 16 payment. Everything else in both
-- Schedules is employee-only. The tribunal is an employees' forum with a narrow
-- counterclaim jurisdiction, and that asymmetry is in the Act rather than in
-- practice.
--
-- s 12(3): the claim must be for "either or both of -- (a) one or more amounts
--   alleged to be payable by the respondent to the claimant; (b) REINSTATEMENT
--   ... in a case where the claim is lodged in respect of a WRONGFUL DISMISSAL
--   DISPUTE." Reinstatement is the only non-money remedy the Act contains, and
--   it is tied to one of the three kinds of dispute.
-- s 12(4): each amount must relate to a specified employment dispute.
-- s 12(5): the claim "must be SUPPORTED BY A CLAIM REFERRAL CERTIFICATE issued
--   in respect of EVERY specified employment dispute for which the claim is
--   lodged."
-- s 12(6): it must be lodged "within the prescribed period after the date of
--   issue of the claim referral certificate" -- a third time limit, and the one
--   the Act does not state.
-- s 12(7): the total alleged must not exceed the prescribed claim limit, counted
--   SEPARATELY for (a) contractual and statutory disputes together and (b)
--   wrongful dismissal disputes.
DECLARE ClaimantKind IS ONE OF
    `a prescribed employee suing their employer`
    `a prescribed employee suing a person liable under Employment Act section 65`
    `a prescribed employer suing their employee`
    `someone the regulations do not prescribe`

DECLARE Claim HAS
    `who is bringing it` IS A ClaimantKind
    `the kind of dispute` IS A DisputeKind
    `the scheduled matter it concerns` IS A ScheduledMatter
    `the matter is one prescribed for section 12(2)(c)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it claims one or more amounts alleged to be payable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it claims reinstatement` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a claim referral certificate was issued for every dispute in the claim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `lodged within the prescribed period after the certificate issued` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the total for contractual and statutory disputes, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `the total for wrongful dismissal disputes, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `the excess over a claim limit has been abandoned under section 15` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE ClaimLimits HAS
    `the limit for contractual and statutory disputes under section 12(7)(a)` IS A NUMBER
    `the limit for wrongful dismissal disputes under section 12(7)(b)` IS A NUMBER

-- s 12(2)(c): the three matters an employer may claim about.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A ScheduledMatter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this is a matter section 12(2)(c)(i) names` IF
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `First Schedule item 17 -- salary in lieu of notice of termination` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Second Schedule item 14 -- the employer's entitlement under Employment Act section 11(1)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Second Schedule item 16 -- the employer's entitlement under Employment Act section 16` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 12(2) is satisfied` IF
    CONSIDER `the claim`'s `who is bringing it`
    WHEN `a prescribed employee suing their employer` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a prescribed employee suing a person liable under Employment Act section 65` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a prescribed employer suing their employee`
        THEN     `this is a matter section 12(2)(c)(i) names` (`the claim`'s `the scheduled matter it concerns`)
             OR  `the claim`'s `the matter is one prescribed for section 12(2)(c)(ii)`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 12(3): reinstatement only on a wrongful dismissal dispute.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 12(3) is satisfied` IF
        `the claim`'s `it claims one or more amounts alleged to be payable`
    OR  (    `the claim`'s `it claims reinstatement`
         AND CONSIDER `the claim`'s `the kind of dispute`
             WHEN `a wrongful dismissal dispute` THEN TRUE
             OTHERWISE FALSE)

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 12(4) is satisfied` IF
    `this is a specified employment dispute` (`the claim`'s `the kind of dispute`)

-- s 12(7), read with s 15(2)(a): abandoning the excess deems the requirement
-- satisfied.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A ClaimLimits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 12(7) is satisfied` IF
        `the claim`'s `the excess over a claim limit has been abandoned under section 15`
    OR  (    `the claim`'s `the total for contractual and statutory disputes, in dollars`
                 AT MOST `the limits`'s `the limit for contractual and statutory disputes under section 12(7)(a)`
         AND `the claim`'s `the total for wrongful dismissal disputes, in dollars`
                 AT MOST `the limits`'s `the limit for wrongful dismissal disputes under section 12(7)(b)`)

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A ClaimLimits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tribunal has jurisdiction` IF
        `section 12(2) is satisfied` `the claim`
    AND `section 12(3) is satisfied` `the claim`
    AND `section 12(4) is satisfied` `the claim`
    AND `the claim`'s `a claim referral certificate was issued for every dispute in the claim`
    AND `the claim`'s `lodged within the prescribed period after the certificate issued`
    AND `section 12(7) is satisfied` `the claim` `the limits`

-- s 12(1)'s closing words: the tribunal's jurisdiction does not depend on when
-- the dispute arose. The time limits are all in s 3(2), s 3(3)(a)(ii) and
-- s 12(6), and none of them is in s 12(1).
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `jurisdiction depends on whether the dispute arose before 1 April 2017` IF
    FALSE

§§ `14 and 15 -- the two caps are separate, and only one can be abandoned down`

-- s 14: "A claim cannot be divided and pursued in separate proceedings before a
-- tribunal IF THE ONLY REASON for doing so is to bring the total amount alleged
-- to be payable in each of those proceedings within the jurisdiction of a
-- tribunal."
--
-- The words "if the only reason" leave room: a division for some other genuine
-- reason is not forbidden by this section.
--
-- s 15(1): where either total exceeds the applicable limit, "the claimant may
-- abandon the excess amount."
-- s 15(2): if the claimant does, "(a) the requirement in section 12(7) is DEEMED
-- to be satisfied; (b) the tribunal HAS jurisdiction; and (c) an order of the
-- tribunal in relation to the claim FULLY DISCHARGES ALL DEMANDS in respect of
-- the claim."
-- s 15(3): "Where the claimant has abandoned the excess amount, the claimant
-- CANNOT RECOVER THAT AMOUNT IN A TRIBUNAL OR ANY OTHER COURT."
--
-- So the price of the tribunal's speed and informality is the excess, and it is
-- paid permanently and everywhere. There is no route by which the claimant takes
-- the limit here and the balance in the District Court: s 15(3) shuts it, and
-- s 16(2) shuts the reverse order while the claim is live.
--
-- s 12(7) counts the two caps separately. A claimant with amounts of both kinds
-- is measured against each limit on its own, so one of the two may be abandoned
-- down while the other is untouched.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A ClaimLimits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the contractual and statutory total exceeds its limit` IF
    `the claim`'s `the total for contractual and statutory disputes, in dollars`
        GREATER THAN `the limits`'s `the limit for contractual and statutory disputes under section 12(7)(a)`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A ClaimLimits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the wrongful dismissal total exceeds its limit` IF
    `the claim`'s `the total for wrongful dismissal disputes, in dollars`
        GREATER THAN `the limits`'s `the limit for wrongful dismissal disputes under section 12(7)(b)`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim, `the limits` IS A ClaimLimits
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the amount abandoned if the claimant abandons the excess` IS
    (IF   `the contractual and statutory total exceeds its limit` `the claim` `the limits`
     THEN `the claim`'s `the total for contractual and statutory disputes, in dollars`
              MINUS `the limits`'s `the limit for contractual and statutory disputes under section 12(7)(a)`
     ELSE 0)
    PLUS
    (IF   `the wrongful dismissal total exceeds its limit` `the claim` `the limits`
     THEN `the claim`'s `the total for wrongful dismissal disputes, in dollars`
              MINUS `the limits`'s `the limit for wrongful dismissal disputes under section 12(7)(b)`
     ELSE 0)

-- s 15(3). Stated as its own function because it is the consequence a claimant
-- is least likely to have been told about.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the abandoned amount can be recovered somewhere else` IF
    FALSE

§§ `16 -- one forum, and the order in which you choose it`

-- s 16(1): a claim cannot be lodged if "(a) any proceedings relating to that
-- claim are pending in, or HAVE BEEN HEARD AND DETERMINED BY, any other court or
-- an Industrial Arbitration Court; or (b) a claim lodged under Employment Act
-- 1968 s 119 in respect of that dispute is pending, or the Commissioner has
-- inquired into and decided that dispute under s 115 of that Act."
--
-- s 16(2): "DESPITE ANY OTHER WRITTEN LAW, where a claim ... is lodged with a
-- tribunal, NO PROCEEDINGS relating to that claim can be commenced in any other
-- court or an Industrial Arbitration Court by either party against the other,
-- unless the claim is withdrawn, discontinued or dismissed for lack of
-- jurisdiction."
--
-- Note which way each subsection cures. s 16(2) lifts if the tribunal claim is
-- withdrawn or discontinued; s 16(1)(a) does not lift if the other proceedings
-- are withdrawn -- it reads "pending in, or have been heard and determined by",
-- so a determined claim bars for good while a withdrawn one stops being pending.
DECLARE OtherProceedings HAS
    `proceedings relating to the claim are pending in another court or the Industrial Arbitration Court` IS A BOOLEAN
    `proceedings relating to the claim have been heard and determined elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a claim under Employment Act section 119 in respect of the dispute is pending` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Commissioner has inquired into and decided the dispute under Employment Act section 115` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the other` IS AN OtherProceedings
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 16(1) bars the claim` IF
        `the other`'s `proceedings relating to the claim are pending in another court or the Industrial Arbitration Court`
    OR  `the other`'s `proceedings relating to the claim have been heard and determined elsewhere`
    OR  `the other`'s `a claim under Employment Act section 119 in respect of the dispute is pending`
    OR  `the other`'s `the Commissioner has inquired into and decided the dispute under Employment Act section 115`

GIVEN `a tribunal claim has been lodged` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the claim was withdrawn, discontinued or dismissed for lack of jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 16(2) bars proceedings elsewhere` IF
        `a tribunal claim has been lodged`
    AND NOT `the claim was withdrawn, discontinued or dismissed for lack of jurisdiction`

§§ `16(3) to 16(6) -- the ministerial-representations trap`

-- Two pairs of subsections, each pair doing the same thing in a different order.
--
-- s 16(3): where an employee is dismissed, the employee CANNOT LODGE a claim for
-- an amount about First Schedule item 17, or Second Schedule item 13 or 15, that
-- arises from that dismissal, if --
--   (a) the employee has made representations in writing under Industrial
--       Relations Act 1960 s 35(3), or under Retirement and Re-employment Act
--       1993 s 8(1), to the Minister; AND
--   (b) either (i) the employee DOES NOT WITHDRAW those representations, or
--       (ii) THE MINISTER CONCERNED MAKES A DECISION on them.
--
-- s 16(5) is the same bar for a claim relating to a WRONGFUL DISMISSAL DISPUTE,
-- on representations under Industrial Relations Act s 35(3) alone.
--
-- s 16(4): where the employee HAS LODGED such a claim and THEN makes those
-- representations, "the claim is DEEMED TO BE DISCONTINUED ... with effect from
-- the date on which the employee makes those representations."
-- s 16(6) is the same for a wrongful dismissal claim.
--
-- THE TWO ORDERS ARE NOT THE SAME. Representations first: the bar in s 16(3)
-- and s 16(5) is conditional, and it lifts if the employee withdraws the
-- representations before the Minister decides -- limb (b) is then satisfied in
-- neither of its two ways. Claim first: s 16(4) and s 16(6) deem the claim
-- discontinued the moment the representations are made, and NOTHING IN EITHER
-- SUBSECTION REVIVES IT IF THE REPRESENTATIONS ARE WITHDRAWN.
--
-- Read with s 3(2)(d) -- one month from dismissal to submit a mediation request
-- for a wrongful dismissal claim -- the second order is usually terminal. By the
-- time an employee has lodged, gone to the Minister and changed their mind, the
-- month is long gone and the discontinued claim cannot be restarted.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A ScheduledMatter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this is a matter section 16(3) and 16(4) name` IF
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `First Schedule item 17 -- salary in lieu of notice of termination` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Second Schedule item 13 -- the employee's entitlement under Employment Act section 11(1)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Second Schedule item 15 -- the employee's entitlement under Employment Act section 16` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE Representations HAS
    `the employee was dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `representations in writing have been made to the Minister under Industrial Relations Act section 35(3)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `representations in writing have been made to the Minister under Retirement and Re-employment Act section 8(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employee withdrew those representations` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Minister concerned has made a decision on those representations` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the representations were made after the claim was lodged` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the representations` IS A Representations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `representations have been made under either provision` IF
        `the representations`'s `representations in writing have been made to the Minister under Industrial Relations Act section 35(3)`
    OR  `the representations`'s `representations in writing have been made to the Minister under Retirement and Re-employment Act section 8(1)`

-- The limb (b) condition, common to s 16(3) and s 16(5).
GIVEN `the representations` IS A Representations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the condition in paragraph (b) is satisfied` IF
        NOT `the representations`'s `the employee withdrew those representations`
    OR  `the representations`'s `the Minister concerned has made a decision on those representations`

GIVEN `the matter` IS A ScheduledMatter, `the representations` IS A Representations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 16(3) bars lodging this claim` IF
        `the representations`'s `the employee was dismissed`
    AND `this is a matter section 16(3) and 16(4) name` `the matter`
    AND `representations have been made under either provision` `the representations`
    AND `the condition in paragraph (b) is satisfied` `the representations`
    AND NOT `the representations`'s `the representations were made after the claim was lodged`

GIVEN `the representations` IS A Representations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 16(5) bars lodging a wrongful dismissal claim` IF
        `the representations`'s `the employee was dismissed`
    AND `the representations`'s `representations in writing have been made to the Minister under Industrial Relations Act section 35(3)`
    AND `the condition in paragraph (b) is satisfied` `the representations`
    AND NOT `the representations`'s `the representations were made after the claim was lodged`

-- s 16(4) and s 16(6). No withdrawal condition, and no revival.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A ScheduledMatter, `the representations` IS A Representations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 16(4) deems the claim discontinued` IF
        `the representations`'s `the employee was dismissed`
    AND `this is a matter section 16(3) and 16(4) name` `the matter`
    AND `representations have been made under either provision` `the representations`
    AND `the representations`'s `the representations were made after the claim was lodged`

GIVEN `the representations` IS A Representations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 16(6) deems a wrongful dismissal claim discontinued` IF
        `the representations`'s `the employee was dismissed`
    AND `the representations`'s `representations in writing have been made to the Minister under Industrial Relations Act section 35(3)`
    AND `the representations`'s `the representations were made after the claim was lodged`

-- The pair of questions that make the trap visible. Whether the employee can end
-- up with a live claim depends only on the order.
GIVEN `the representations` IS A Representations
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a wrongful dismissal claim can still be pursued` IF
        NOT `section 16(5) bars lodging a wrongful dismissal claim` `the representations`
    AND NOT `section 16(6) deems a wrongful dismissal claim discontinued` `the representations`

§§ `21 -- what the tribunal may do about evidence`

-- s 21(1): the tribunal "(a) is NOT BOUND BY THE RULES OF EVIDENCE ... and
--   (b) may inform itself on any matter in any manner that the tribunal thinks
--   fit."
-- s 21(2): the tribunal "may draw any inferences that the tribunal thinks fit
--   from a party's FAILURE TO COMPLY WITH ANY OBLIGATION of that party under any
--   written law specified in the Fourth Schedule, including ... an inference
--   that any evidence that is not available on account of that failure would, if
--   produced, have been UNFAVOURABLE to that party."
-- s 21(3) and (4): evidence need not be on oath, but the tribunal may require it.
-- s 21(5) and (6): the tribunal may seek its own evidence, and must disclose
--   whatever it obtains to every party.
-- s 21(8): the tribunal "need not keep a record of the evidence", but must make
--   a summary of the facts and issues and notes of the proceedings.
--
-- s 21(2) is the practical answer to the commonest problem in a small wage
-- claim: the employer holds the records. An employer who has not kept the
-- records the Employment Act requires can have the adverse inference drawn
-- against them. Note that it is a power and not a presumption -- "may draw any
-- inferences that the tribunal thinks fit" -- and that it is tied to the Fourth
-- Schedule list of written laws, not to record-keeping generally.
--
-- s 21(8) is worth reading beside s 23(1): an appeal lies on a question of law,
-- and the tribunal need not have recorded the evidence on which the facts were
-- found.
GIVEN `the party failed to comply with an obligation under a written law in the Fourth Schedule` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tribunal may draw an adverse inference under section 21(2)` IF
    `the party failed to comply with an obligation under a written law in the Fourth Schedule`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 21(2) creates a presumption against the party` IF
    FALSE

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the tribunal must keep a record of the evidence` IF
    FALSE

§§ `23 -- appeal`

-- s 23(1): any party may appeal to the General Division of the High Court
-- against an order under s 22(1) "(a) on any ground involving A QUESTION OF LAW;
-- or (b) on the ground that the claim was OUTSIDE THE JURISDICTION of the
-- tribunal."
-- s 23(2): "An appeal lies ... ONLY IF PERMISSION TO APPEAL IS GIVEN BY A
--   DISTRICT COURT."
-- s 23(4): the District Court's order giving or refusing permission "is FINAL
--   and is not subject to any appeal."
--
-- So there is no appeal on the facts, and the gate to the appeal that does exist
-- is itself unappealable. A party who says the tribunal got the facts wrong has
-- nowhere to go; a party who says it got the law wrong must first persuade a
-- District Court, whose refusal is the end of the matter.
DECLARE Appeal HAS
    `the ground involves a question of law` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the ground is that the claim was outside the tribunal's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the ground is that the tribunal found the facts wrongly` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a District Court has given permission to appeal` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the appeal` IS AN Appeal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the ground is one section 23(1) allows` IF
        `the appeal`'s `the ground involves a question of law`
    OR  `the appeal`'s `the ground is that the claim was outside the tribunal's jurisdiction`

GIVEN `the appeal` IS AN Appeal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an appeal lies to the General Division of the High Court` IF
        `the ground is one section 23(1) allows` `the appeal`
    AND `the appeal`'s `a District Court has given permission to appeal`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a refusal of permission to appeal can itself be appealed` IF
    FALSE

-- s 24(1): "An appeal ... does not operate as a stay of execution or enforcement
-- of that order, unless a District Court or the General Division of the High
-- Court orders otherwise."
GIVEN `a court has ordered a stay` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order may be enforced while the appeal is pending` IF
    NOT `a court has ordered a stay`

§§ `27 -- who has to prove what`

-- s 27(1): a person who alleges that they are NOT the employer or employee of
-- the claimant, or NOT a person with a duty under the Child Development
-- Co-Savings Act 2001, the Employment Act 1968 or the Retirement and
-- Re-employment Act 1993, "BEARS THE BURDEN OF PROVING that allegation."
--
-- s 27(2): the employer bears the burden --
--   (a) where an employee dismissed without notice under Employment Act s 14(1)
--       lodges a s 14(2) claim: of proving the dismissal was "with just cause or
--       excuse";
--   (b) where an employee is dismissed WITH NOTICE and "the notice of dismissal
--       IS OR PURPORTS TO BE GIVEN ON THE GROUND THAT THERE HAS BEEN POOR
--       PERFORMANCE OR MISCONDUCT": of proving that ground;
--   (c) and (d) where a female employee is dismissed in the s 84(1)
--       circumstances: of proving sufficient cause.
--
-- Limb (b) is conditional on the employer having stated a reason. An employer
-- who gives contractual notice and says nothing about why does not come within
-- it; an employer who writes "for poor performance" takes on the burden of
-- proving poor performance. The incentive the subsection creates is to say
-- nothing, which is the opposite of what an employee needs.
DECLARE BurdenFacts HAS
    `the person alleges they are not the employer or employee of the claimant` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person alleges they have no duty under one of the three named Acts` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employee was dismissed without notice under Employment Act section 14(1)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employee lodged a claim under Employment Act section 14(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employee was dismissed with notice` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the notice is or purports to be given on the ground of poor performance or misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a notice of dismissal was given to a female employee in the section 84(1) circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the female employee lodged a claim under Employment Act section 84(2)` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the facts` IS A BurdenFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person making the allegation bears the burden under section 27(1)` IF
        `the facts`'s `the person alleges they are not the employer or employee of the claimant`
    OR  `the facts`'s `the person alleges they have no duty under one of the three named Acts`

GIVEN `the facts` IS A BurdenFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer bears the burden on the reason for the dismissal` IF
        (    `the facts`'s `the employee was dismissed without notice under Employment Act section 14(1)`
         AND `the facts`'s `the employee lodged a claim under Employment Act section 14(2)`)
    OR  (    `the facts`'s `the employee was dismissed with notice`
         AND `the facts`'s `the notice is or purports to be given on the ground of poor performance or misconduct`)
    OR  (    `the facts`'s `a notice of dismissal was given to a female employee in the section 84(1) circumstances`
         AND `the facts`'s `the female employee lodged a claim under Employment Act section 84(2)`)

§§ `28 -- contracting out`

-- s 28: "Any provision in any agreement (whether made before, on or after
-- 1 April 2017) is VOID to the extent that it purports -- (a) to exclude or
-- limit the jurisdiction of a tribunal; or (b) to prevent a person from
-- (i) submitting a mediation request; or (ii) making a claim, an application or
-- an appeal under this Act."
--
-- "To the extent that" -- severance, not wholesale invalidity. And the section
-- reaches an arbitration clause, a settlement that purports to bar a future
-- claim, and an employment term requiring disputes to go elsewhere.
DECLARE AgreementTerm HAS
    `purports to exclude or limit the jurisdiction of a tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN
    `purports to prevent a person from submitting a mediation request` IS A BOOLEAN
    `purports to prevent a person from making a claim, application or appeal under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the agreement was made before 1 April 2017` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the term` IS AN AgreementTerm
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the term is void under section 28` IF
        `the term`'s `purports to exclude or limit the jurisdiction of a tribunal`
    OR  `the term`'s `purports to prevent a person from submitting a mediation request`
    OR  `the term`'s `purports to prevent a person from making a claim, application or appeal under this Act`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the date of the agreement makes any difference under section 28` IF
    FALSE
